"""Independent notes and examples, following the mathematical sequence of sections 8.1–8.4."""
import sympy as s
x,y=s.symbols('x y',real=True)
def val(a):return s.sympify(a,locals={'x':x,'y':y})
def tex(a):return s.latex(val(a))
def inline(a):return r'\('+tex(a)+r'\)'
def display(a):return r'\['+a+r'\]'
def cases(name,var,pieces,cdf=False,open_left=False):
    rows=[]
    if cdf:rows.append('0,&amp;'+var+r'\lt '+tex(pieces[0][1]))
    for index,(f,a,b) in enumerate(pieces):rows.append(tex(f)+',&amp;'+tex(a)+(r'\lt ' if open_left and index==0 else r'\le ')+var+(r'\lt ' if cdf or index<len(pieces)-1 else r'\le ')+tex(b))
    rows.append(('1,&amp;'+var+r'\ge '+tex(pieces[-1][2])) if cdf else r'0,&amp;\text{otherwise}')
    return display(name+'('+var+r')=\begin{cases}'+r'\\'.join(rows)+r'.\end{cases}')
models=[]
def ex(code,title,question,solution,pdf,**kwargs):
    models.append(dict(id=code,pdf=pdf,**kwargs))
    return dict(title=title,question=question,solution=solution,verification=code)
def lesson(key,title,summary,objective,before,ideas,method,mistakes,examples):return dict(key=key,id='stats_crv_'+key,title=title,summary=summary,objective=objective,before=before,ideas=ideas,method=method,mistakes=mistakes,examples=examples)
base='/courses/maths4u-9231-statistics/topics/stats_crv_'
lessons=[
lesson('density','Density models and probability areas',
 'Separate a curve height from probability, and use the complete support.',
 'Validate and normalise piecewise densities and calculate probabilities as areas.',
 r'<p>Review definite integration, non-negative functions and interval notation. Some written questions continue into <a href="'+base+r'cdf">CDFs and quantiles</a> and <a href="'+base+r'moments">moments</a>; return to those parts after the relevant lesson.</p>',
 r'''<h3>A height is not a point probability</h3><p>A density f distributes probability across intervals. For a variable with a density,</p>
 \[\mathrm P(a\le X\le b)=\int_a^b f(x)\,dx.\]
 <p>The value f(a) is a height, whereas the probability of the single point X=a is zero. A density may exceed 1; its total area, rather than its maximum height, must be 1. Adding or removing one endpoint from an event does not change its probability.</p>
 <h3>Two tests are needed</h3>\[f(x)\ge0\quad\text{on the support},\qquad\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}f(x)\,dx=1.\]
 <p>Normalisation alone does not rule out a negative section. Solve for unknown coefficients, then check the sign on every piece. Values outside the stated support contribute no probability.</p>
 <h3>Piecewise does not mean continuous</h3><p>Split an integral at every formula change and at any gap in the support. A continuous random variable need not have a continuous density. Equate adjacent density heights only when the question explicitly imposes that extra condition. A density's value at an isolated endpoint may be reassigned without changing the model.</p>
 <h3>Read the model before naming a distribution</h3><p>If an exponential expression is supplied, obtain its probabilities by integration or from the supplied CDF. No memorised mean, median or named-distribution property is required in this chapter.</p>''',
 r'<ol><li>Write all support intervals and formula changes.</li><li>Impose total area 1 and any stated area or matching conditions.</li><li>Check each resulting piece is non-negative.</li><li>Intersect the requested event with the support and split the integral.</li><li>Check the resulting probability lies between 0 and 1.</li></ol>',
 ['A density height can exceed 1; a probability cannot.','A single point has probability zero, not f at that point.','Do not impose continuity of the density unless it is stated.','Do not integrate one branch across a different branch or a gap.'],
 [ex('E-D1','1 · Normalise before using a height',r'<p>Let \(f(x)=k(2+x)\) for \(0\le x\le1\), and zero otherwise. Find k, f(1), and \(\mathrm P(\frac14\lt X\lt\frac34)\).</p>',
 r'<p>Total area gives \(k[2x+x^2/2]_0^1=5k/2=1\), so \(k=2/5\). The height \(f(1)=6/5\) exceeds 1 and is permitted. The interval probability is</p>\[\frac25[2x+x^2/2]_{1/4}^{3/4}=\frac12.\]<p>The probability at the point X=1 itself is zero.</p>',[('2*(2+x)/5',0,1)],checks=[dict(kind='normalisation',expression='5*k/2-1',parameter='k',answer='2/5'),dict(kind='density_value',at=1,answer='6/5'),dict(kind='probability',lower='1/4',upper='3/4',answer='1/2')]),
 ex('E-D2','2 · Solve areas without inventing a matching condition',r'<p>A density is \(ax\) on \([0,1)\), b on \([1,3]\), and zero elsewhere. Given \(\mathrm P(X\lt1)=\frac13\), find a and b. Must the two density heights agree at 1?</p>',
 r'<p>The first area is a/2=1/3, so a=2/3. Total area gives a/2+2b=1, hence b=1/3. Both branches are non-negative. The left-hand height at 1 is 2/3, while the right-hand height is 1/3. A jump in this density is allowed; no matching condition was given.</p>',[('2*x/3',0,1),('1/3',1,3)],checks=[dict(kind='probability',lower=0,upper=1,answer='1/3'),dict(kind='density_jump',at=1,left='2/3',right='1/3')]),
 ex('E-D3','3 · Use the correct part of a curved density',r'<p>The density is \(cx(4-x)\) on \([0,4]\), zero otherwise. Find c and \(\mathrm P(X\gt3)\).</p>',
 r'<p>The integral of x(4−x) on [0,4] is 32/3, so c=3/32. The factors x and 4−x are non-negative throughout the support. Thus</p>\[\mathrm P(X>3)=\frac3{32}[2x^2-x^3/3]_3^4=\frac5{32}.\]<p>This is an area; substituting x=3 into the density would not give the requested probability.</p>',[('3*x*(4-x)/32',0,4)],checks=[dict(kind='normalisation',expression='32*k/3-1',parameter='k',answer='3/32'),dict(kind='probability',lower=3,upper=4,answer='5/32')]),
 ex('E-D4','4 · An event can cross an empty interval',r'<p>A density is 2 on \([0,\frac14]\), \(\frac12\) on \([1,2]\), and zero otherwise. Check it is valid and find \(\mathrm P(\frac15\lt X\lt\frac65)\).</p>',
 r'<p>Both pieces are positive and their areas are 1/2 and 1/2. For the event, only [1/5,1/4] and [1,6/5] contribute:</p>\[2\left(\frac14-\frac15\right)+\frac12\left(\frac65-1\right)=\frac15.\]<p>The gap (1/4,1) contributes zero; it is not filled by either formula.</p>',[('2',0,'1/4'),('1/2',1,2)],checks=[dict(kind='probability',lower='1/5',upper='6/5',answer='1/5')])]),
lesson('cdf','CDFs, quantiles and conditional probabilities',
 'Accumulate probability across each piece, then choose the correct branch of an inverse problem.',
 'Build complete CDFs and recover densities, percentiles and conditional probabilities.',
 '<p>Complete <a href="'+base+'density">density models</a>. Review definite integration, quadratic equations, logarithms and conditional probability.</p>',
 r'''<h3>A complete cumulative description</h3>\[F(x)=\mathrm P(X\le x)=\int_{-\infty}^x f(t)\,dt.\]
 <p>For a finite support [a,b], give F=0 below a and F=1 above b, as well as each interior branch. On the second piece, retain all the mass accumulated on earlier pieces. The dummy integration variable t keeps the upper limit x distinct.</p>
 <p>A CDF is non-decreasing and lies in [0,1]. For a distribution with a density it is continuous, even where the density jumps. Where differentiable, F′=f. A corner or an arbitrary value of f at one point does not create a point mass.</p>
 <h3>Probabilities from cumulative values</h3>\[\mathrm P(a<X\le b)=F(b)-F(a),\qquad\mathrm P(X>b)=1-F(b).\]
 <p>For example, with a&lt;b inside the support, the conditional upper-tail probability is \((1-F(b))/(1-F(a))\), provided the denominator is positive. Derive it from the event intersection rather than using an unstated model property.</p>
 <h3>Select a branch before solving</h3><p>A p-quantile q satisfies F(q)=p for a strictly increasing continuous CDF; a median has p=1/2. Compare p with the cumulative masses at the breakpoints, solve within the correct branch, and reject roots outside it. For a plateau, use the lower-quantile convention \(q_p=\inf\{x:F(x)\ge p\}\); a flat section may mean the equation has more than one solution.</p>
 <p>The interquartile range is \(q_{0.75}-q_{0.25}\). Solve each quartile separately, since they may lie in different pieces. Give exact values unless a rounding instruction is present.</p>''',
 r'<ol><li>Integrate from the left support boundary.</li><li>Carry accumulated mass into every later piece.</li><li>Include exterior branches and check continuity, endpoints and monotonicity.</li><li>For a percentile, identify its branch using breakpoint probabilities.</li><li>For a conditional event, divide the intersection probability by the conditioning probability.</li></ol>',
 ['The integration constant changes between pieces to preserve accumulated mass.','Differentiate a CDF; integrate a density.','A percentile is an x-value, not a probability.','A quartile root must belong to the branch used to obtain it.'],
 [ex('E-C1','1 · Give the exterior branches as well',r'<p>Let \(f(x)=\frac{2x+1}{6}\) on \([0,2]\), zero elsewhere. Find its CDF and \(\mathrm P(1\lt X\le\frac32)\).</p>',
 cases('F','x',[('(x**2+x)/6',0,2)],True)+r'<p>The interior integral is \(\int_0^x(2t+1)/6\,dt\). The requested probability is \(F(3/2)-F(1)=5/8-1/3=7/24\). F(0)=0 and F(2)=1 verify both joins.</p>',[('(2*x+1)/6',0,2)],cdf=[('(x**2+x)/6',0,2)],checks=[dict(kind='probability',lower=1,upper='3/2',answer='7/24')]),
 ex('E-C2','2 · Quartiles on different pieces',r'<p>A density is x on \([0,1)\), \(\frac14\) on \([1,3]\), and zero elsewhere. Find the interquartile range.</p>',
 cases('F','x',[('x**2/2',0,1),('(x+1)/4',1,3)],True)+r'<p>F(1)=1/2. The lower quartile therefore uses x²/2=1/4, giving \(q_{0.25}=\sqrt2/2\). The upper uses (x+1)/4=3/4, giving \(q_{0.75}=2\). Hence the interquartile range is \(2-\sqrt2/2\). Both roots are in their selected branches.</p>',[('x',0,1),('1/4',1,3)],cdf=[('x**2/2',0,1),('(x+1)/4',1,3)],checks=[dict(kind='quantile',p='1/4',answer='sqrt(2)/2'),dict(kind='quantile',p='3/4',answer=2)]),
 ex('E-C3','3 · Recover a density from an interior CDF',r'<p>The CDF is 0 below 1, \((x-1)^2/9\) on \([1,4)\), and 1 at and above 4. Find the density and \(\mathrm P(2\lt X\le3)\).</p>',
 cases('f','x',[('2*(x-1)/9',1,4)])+r'<p>Differentiation gives the interior density; it is zero elsewhere. The CDF is continuous at both support endpoints, so no atom is present. The interval probability is \(4/9-1/9=1/3\).</p>',[('2*(x-1)/9',1,4)],cdf=[('(x-1)**2/9',1,4)],checks=[dict(kind='probability',lower=2,upper=3,answer='1/3')]),
 ex('E-C4','4 · Work directly with a supplied exponential expression',r'<p>A reset-cycle duration T has CDF 0 for t&lt;0 and \(1-e^{-t/4}\) for t≥0. Find its 90th percentile and \(\mathrm P(T\gt8\mid T\gt4)\).</p>',
 r'<p>The percentile equation is \(1-e^{-q/4}=9/10\). Thus \(e^{-q/4}=1/10\), giving \(q=4\ln10\). The conditional event is obtained from the two tails:</p>\[\frac{\mathrm P(T>8)}{\mathrm P(T>4)}=\frac{e^{-2}}{e^{-1}}=e^{-1}.\]<p>The denominator is positive. No named-distribution property was assumed.</p>',[('exp(-x/4)/4',0,'oo')],cdf=[('1-exp(-x/4)',0,'oo')],checks=[dict(kind='quantile',p='9/10',answer='4*log(10)'),dict(kind='conditional',lower=8,given=4,answer='exp(-1)')])]),
lesson('moments','Expectations of functions and variance',
 'Integrate the requested function against the original density, checking the whole support.',
 'Calculate expectations and variances of functions without confusing a function of a mean with a mean of a function.',
 '<p>Review <a href="'+base+'density">normalised densities</a>, integration of powers, and improper integrals where the support or integrand requires them.</p>',
 r'''<h3>The density still belongs to X</h3>\[\mathrm E(h(X))=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}h(x)f_X(x)\,dx.\]
 <p>Use every support piece, replacing X by the integration variable x inside h. This method needs no inverse for h and works even when h is not monotone. The expectation exists as a finite real value when the absolute integral is finite; do not ignore a singularity or an infinite tail.</p>
 <h3>Variance needs two moments of the same variable</h3>\[\operatorname{Var}(h(X))=\mathrm E(h(X)^2)-\bigl(\mathrm E(h(X))\bigr)^2.\]
 <p>For h(X)=√X, the first term is E(X). For h(X)=X², it is E(X⁴), not E(X²). A negative variance signals an error, but a positive result alone is not proof that the calculation is correct.</p>
 <h3>Only affine functions commute with expectation in general</h3>\[\mathrm E(aX+b)=a\mathrm E(X)+b,\qquad\operatorname{Var}(aX+b)=a^2\operatorname{Var}(X).\]
 <p>In general E(h(X)) does not equal h(E(X)). Likewise Var(√X) is not √Var(X), and E(1/X) is not 1/E(X). When applying a given moment, check whether it is the first or second moment of the variable actually requested.</p>''',
 r'<ol><li>Name the transformed quantity h(X).</li><li>Integrate h(x)f(x) on every original support interval.</li><li>For variance, separately integrate h(x)²f(x).</li><li>Check convergence and combine the moments exactly.</li><li>Round only the final requested quantity.</li></ol>',
 ['Do not substitute h(x) into the density in place of multiplying by it.','Var(X²) needs the fourth moment of X.','E(√X) is generally not √E(X).','A singular integrand requires a convergence check, even if a single point has probability zero.'],
 [ex('E-M1','1 · Keep the first and second moments separate',r'<p>Let \(f(x)=\frac{3x^2}{8}\) on \([0,2]\), zero elsewhere. Find \(\mathrm E(X)\) and \(\operatorname{Var}(X)\).</p>',
 r'\[\mathrm E(X)=\frac38\int_0^2x^3\,dx=\frac32,\qquad\mathrm E(X^2)=\frac38\int_0^2x^4\,dx=\frac{12}5.\]\[\operatorname{Var}(X)=\frac{12}5-\left(\frac32\right)^2=\frac3{20}.\]<p>The density integrates to 1. Both moments are finite on its bounded support.</p>',[('3*x**2/8',0,2)],checks=[dict(kind='moment',function='x',answer='3/2'),dict(kind='moment',function='x**2',answer='12/5'),dict(kind='variance',function='x',answer='3/20')]),
 ex('E-M2','2 · Variance after a square root',r'<p>Let \(f(x)=\frac{2(x+1)}{15}\) on \([0,3]\), zero elsewhere. Find \(\mathrm E(\sqrt X)\) and \(\operatorname{Var}(\sqrt X)\).</p>',
 r'\[\mathrm E(\sqrt X)=\frac2{15}\int_0^3(x^{3/2}+x^{1/2})\,dx=\frac{56\sqrt3}{75}.\]<p>Since (√X)²=X, the other moment is E(X)=9/5. Hence</p>\[\operatorname{Var}(\sqrt X)=\frac95-\left(\frac{56\sqrt3}{75}\right)^2=\frac{239}{1875}.\]<p>Integrating the fractional power is valid at 0: its absolute integral is finite.</p>',[('2*(x+1)/15',0,3)],checks=[dict(kind='moment',function='sqrt(x)',answer='56*sqrt(3)/75'),dict(kind='moment',function='x',answer='9/5'),dict(kind='variance',function='sqrt(x)',answer='239/1875')]),
 ex('E-M3','3 · A reciprocal with a safe lower endpoint',r'<p>Let \(f(x)=\frac{2x}{3}\) on \([1,2]\), zero elsewhere. Find \(\mathrm E(1/X)\) and \(\operatorname{Var}(1/X)\).</p>',
 r'\[\mathrm E(1/X)=\int_1^2\frac1x\frac{2x}3\,dx=\frac23,\qquad\mathrm E(1/X^2)=\frac23\int_1^2\frac1x\,dx=\frac{2\ln2}3.\]\[\operatorname{Var}(1/X)=\frac{2\ln2}3-\frac49.\]<p>The support stays above zero, so neither reciprocal has a singularity within the integration interval.</p>',[('2*x/3',1,2)],checks=[dict(kind='moment',function='1/x',answer='2/3'),dict(kind='moment',function='1/x**2',answer='2*log(2)/3'),dict(kind='variance',function='1/x',answer='2*log(2)/3-4/9')]),
 ex('E-M4','4 · No inverse is needed for an expectation',r'<p>X is uniform on \([0,2]\), and \(Y=(X-1)^2\). Find \(\mathrm E(Y)\) and \(\operatorname{Var}(Y)\).</p>',
 r'\[\mathrm E(Y)=\frac12\int_0^2(x-1)^2\,dx=\frac13,\qquad\mathrm E(Y^2)=\frac12\int_0^2(x-1)^4\,dx=\frac15.\]\[\operatorname{Var}(Y)=\frac15-\frac19=\frac4{45}.\]<p>The function is not monotone on [0,2], but the direct expectation formula does not require monotonicity. In contrast, (E(X)−1)²=0 is not E(Y).</p>',[('1/2',0,2)],checks=[dict(kind='moment',function='(x-1)**2',answer='1/3'),dict(kind='moment',function='(x-1)**4',answer='1/5'),dict(kind='variance',function='(x-1)**2',answer='4/45')])]),
lesson('monotone','Increasing and decreasing transformations',
 'Transform the event and support before differentiating a new CDF.',
 'Derive complete CDFs and densities for monotone related variables, reversing inequalities when needed.',
 '<p>Complete <a href="'+base+'cdf">CDFs and quantiles</a>. Review inverse functions, the chain rule, logarithms and inequalities under decreasing functions.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Begin with the event</h3><p>Let Y=h(X) and write G(y)=P(Y≤y). First find the range of h on the support of X. Only then invert the inequality for interior y-values.</p>
 <p>If h is increasing, \(h(X)\le y\) means \(X\le h^{-1}(y)\), so</p>\[G(y)=F_X(h^{-1}(y)).\]
 <p>If h is decreasing, the event becomes \(X\ge h^{-1}(y)\). For a variable with a density, the equality endpoint has no mass, so</p>\[G(y)=1-F_X(h^{-1}(y)).\]
 <h3>Differentiate after stating the range</h3><p>Give G=0 below the lower endpoint and G=1 at and above the upper endpoint. Differentiate the appropriate interior expression to obtain the density g. The chain-rule factor is essential; a substitution into f alone does not transform an area correctly.</p>
 <p>As a check after the CDF derivation, \(g(y)=f_X(h^{-1}(y))|(h^{-1})'(y)|\) on a monotone branch. The absolute value makes a decreasing transformation's density non-negative. This is a check, not a replacement for the CDF argument required in the written questions.</p>
 <h3>Check endpoints and percentiles</h3><p>Map both support endpoints, including limiting endpoints for an infinite original support. In a decreasing map, their order reverses. Solve G(q)=p for a transformed percentile; an increasing map transports the same percentile, whereas a decreasing map reverses cumulative probabilities.</p>''',
 r'<ol><li>Find the transformed support and any mapped breakpoints.</li><li>Write G(y)=P(h(X)≤y).</li><li>Invert the event, reversing the inequality for a decreasing function.</li><li>Insert the complete original CDF and state exterior values.</li><li>Differentiate and check non-negativity and total mass.</li></ol>',
 ['Substituting into a density omits the chain-rule factor.','A decreasing inverse requires a tail probability, not the original CDF value.','Transform the support as well as the formula.','The CDF must increase even when the transformation decreases.'],
 [ex('E-T1','1 · An affine change still needs a scale factor',r'<p>X has density \(6x(1-x)\) on \([0,1]\). Let \(Y=2X+1\). Find G and g.</p>',
 cases('G','y',[('3*((y-1)/2)**2-2*((y-1)/2)**3',1,3)],True)+cases('g','y',[('3*(y-1)*(3-y)/4',1,3)])+r'<p>For 1≤y≤3, Y≤y is X≤(y−1)/2. Integrating the original density gives F(x)=3x²−2x³. Substitute the inverse into F, then differentiate; the inverse contributes a factor 1/2. The resulting density integrates to 1.</p>',[('6*x*(1-x)',0,1)],transform=dict(h='(y-1)/2',direction=1,support=[1,3]),transformed_cdf=[('3*((y-1)/2)**2-2*((y-1)/2)**3',1,3)],transformed_pdf=[('3*(y-1)*(3-y)/4',1,3)]),
 ex('E-T2','2 · A nonlinear map can produce a constant density',r'<p>X has density \(\frac x4\) on \([1,3]\). Let \(Y=X^2+2\). Find G and g.</p>',
 cases('G','y',[('(y-3)/8',3,11)],True)+cases('g','y',[('1/8',3,11)])+r'<p>The map is increasing on the entire support, with range [3,11]. For interior y, G(y)=F(√(y−2)). Since F(x)=(x²−1)/8, the substituted CDF is (y−3)/8. Differentiation gives 1/8; its area on [3,11] is 1.</p>',[('x/4',1,3)],transform=dict(h='sqrt(y-2)',direction=1,support=[3,11]),transformed_cdf=[('(y-3)/8',3,11)],transformed_pdf=[('1/8',3,11)]),
 ex('E-T3','3 · A reciprocal reverses the event',r'<p>X has density \(\frac{2x}{15}\) on \([1,4]\). Let \(Y=1/X\). Find G and g.</p>',
 cases('G','y',[('(16-1/y**2)/15','1/4',1)],True)+cases('g','y',[('2/(15*y**3)','1/4',1)])+r'<p>The support becomes [1/4,1]. For interior y, 1/X≤y is X≥1/y. Therefore G(y)=1−F(1/y), with F(x)=(x²−1)/15. Differentiating the tail expression gives a positive density; using F(1/y) alone would produce a decreasing function.</p>',[('2*x/15',1,4)],transform=dict(h='1/y',direction=-1,support=['1/4',1]),transformed_cdf=[('(16-1/y**2)/15','1/4',1)],transformed_pdf=[('2/(15*y**3)','1/4',1)]),
 ex('E-T4','4 · A logarithm transforms the endpoints too',r'<p>X is uniform on \([1,5]\), and \(Y=\ln X\). Find the density of Y and \(\mathrm P(Y\le\ln3)\).</p>',
 cases('G','y',[('(exp(y)-1)/4',0,'log(5)')],True)+cases('g','y',[('exp(y)/4',0,'log(5)')])+r'<p>The inverse is \(e^y\) and the range is [0,ln 5]. Thus \(G(y)=\mathrm P(X\le e^y)=(e^y-1)/4\) on that range. The required probability is \(G(\ln3)=(3-1)/4=1/2\).</p>',[('1/4',1,5)],transform=dict(h='exp(y)',direction=1,support=[0,'log(5)']),transformed_cdf=[('(exp(y)-1)/4',0,'log(5)')],transformed_pdf=[('exp(y)/4',0,'log(5)')],checks=[dict(kind='transformed_probability',lower=0,upper='log(3)',answer='1/2')])]),
lesson('piecewise','Mapped breakpoints and multiple inverse branches',
 'Keep every contributing interval when a model or transformation changes formula.',
 'Derive piecewise transformed distributions from complete events and correctly mapped breakpoints.',
 '<p>Complete <a href="'+base+'monotone">monotone transformations</a> and <a href="'+base+'cdf">piecewise CDFs</a>. The two multi-branch examples extend the same CDF method; no joint densities are used.</p>',
 r'''<h3>A breakpoint belongs to the variable being transformed</h3><p>If the original density or CDF changes at x=c, then the corresponding transformed formula changes at y=h(c). For a monotone map, carry every original piece and any support gap through the inverse in the correct order.</p>
 <h3>Do not restart the accumulated mass</h3><p>A transformed CDF must join continuously across its interior breakpoints. Its density may jump. Test each CDF branch at both adjacent boundaries, and check that the final branch reaches 1. A gap remains a flat section of the CDF, rather than a new atom.</p>
 <h3>For a non-monotone map, invert the whole event</h3><p>An inverse may have more than one branch. For Y=X² and y≥0,</p>\[G(y)=\mathrm P(-\sqrt y\le X\le\sqrt y).\]
 <p>Intersect that interval with the actual support. If a boundary crosses a support endpoint, the contributing intervals change. Similarly, |X−c|≤y describes an interval [c−y,c+y], not one inverse point. Form its probability first, then differentiate each resulting y-branch.</p>
 <h3>Endpoint singularities can be integrable</h3><p>A transformed density may become unbounded near an endpoint while retaining finite area 1. Check its improper integral; a large or infinite limiting height is not a point mass. Assigning a density value at the single endpoint does not change the CDF.</p>''',
 r'<ol><li>Map every original endpoint and breakpoint.</li><li>For each y-range, solve the full event h(X)≤y.</li><li>Intersect with all original support pieces.</li><li>Calculate the cumulative probability, retaining earlier mass.</li><li>Check joins, differentiate by branch, and verify total mass.</li></ol>',
 ['A transformed breakpoint is h(c), not c.','A square root used as an inverse may hide a second preimage.','The lower and upper branches may contribute together.','A density singularity is not an atom if its area remains finite.'],
 [ex('E-B1','1 · Move the join with the transformation',r'<p>The density is \(\frac14\) on \([0,2)\), \(\frac{4-x}{4}\) on \([2,4]\), and zero elsewhere. For \(Y=\sqrt{X+1}\), find G and g.</p>',
 cases('G','y',[('(y**2-1)/4',1,'sqrt(3)'),('1-(5-y**2)**2/8','sqrt(3)','sqrt(5)')],True)+cases('g','y',[('y/2',1,'sqrt(3)'),('y*(5-y**2)/2','sqrt(3)','sqrt(5)')])+r'<p>The range is [1,√5], and x=2 maps to y=√3. The inverse event is X≤y²−1. Substitute into the correct CDF branch: F(x)=x/4 before 2 and F(x)=1−(4−x)²/8 afterwards. Both transformed branches give 1/2 at √3.</p>',[('1/4',0,2),('(4-x)/4',2,4)],transform=dict(h='y**2-1',direction=1,support=[1,'sqrt(3)','sqrt(5)']),transformed_cdf=[('(y**2-1)/4',1,'sqrt(3)'),('1-(5-y**2)**2/8','sqrt(3)','sqrt(5)')],transformed_pdf=[('y/2',1,'sqrt(3)'),('y*(5-y**2)/2','sqrt(3)','sqrt(5)')]),
 ex('E-B2','2 · A square includes both signs',r'<p>A density is \(\frac{2(x+1)}3\) on \([-1,0)\), \(\frac{4(1-x)}3\) on \([0,1]\), and zero elsewhere. Find the distribution of \(Y=X^2\).</p>',
 cases('G','y',[('2*sqrt(y)-y',0,1)],True)+cases('g','y',[('1/sqrt(y)-1',0,1)],open_left=True)+r'<p>For \(0\le y\le1\), integrate from \(-\sqrt y\) to \(\sqrt y\), splitting at 0. The left and right contributions sum to \(2\sqrt y-y\). Differentiating gives \(y^{-1/2}-1\) on (0,1). Its improper integral is \([2\sqrt y-y]_0^1=1\). The infinite limiting height near 0 creates no atom because G is continuous there; its value at that single endpoint may be assigned arbitrarily.</p>',[('2*(x+1)/3',-1,0),('4*(1-x)/3',0,1)],two_sided_square=True,transformed_cdf=[('2*sqrt(y)-y',0,1)],transformed_pdf=[('1/sqrt(y)-1',0,1)]),
 ex('E-B3','3 · A decreasing map preserves a gap',r'<p>A density is \(\frac12\) on \([0,1]\) and \([2,3]\), zero elsewhere. Let \(Y=4-X\). Find its CDF.</p>',
 cases('G','y',[('(y-1)/2',1,2),('1/2',2,3),('(y-2)/2',3,4)],True)+r'<p>The transformed support is [1,2] together with [3,4]. The event X≥4−y uses the original upper tail. Between y=2 and y=3 the CDF stays at 1/2 because no transformed values lie in that gap. Both joins are continuous; the density is zero on the gap.</p>',[('1/2',0,1),('0',1,2),('1/2',2,3)],transform=dict(h='4-y',direction=-1,support=[1,2,3,4]),transformed_cdf=[('(y-1)/2',1,2),('1/2',2,3),('(y-2)/2',3,4)]),
 ex('E-B4','4 · The number of inverse branches can change',r'<p>X is uniform on \([0,3]\), and \(Y=|X-1|\). Find G, g and \(\mathrm P(Y\le\frac32)\).</p>',
 cases('G','y',[('2*y/3',0,1),('(y+1)/3',1,2)],True)+cases('g','y',[('2/3',0,1),('1/3',1,2)])+r'<p>The event is 1−y≤X≤1+y, clipped to [0,3]. For 0≤y&lt;1 its length is 2y. For 1≤y≤2 it runs from 0 to 1+y, with length 1+y. Divide each by 3. The CDF joins at 2/3, and G(3/2)=5/6.</p>',[('1/3',0,3)],absolute=dict(centre=1,upper=3),transformed_cdf=[('2*y/3',0,1),('(y+1)/3',1,2)],transformed_pdf=[('2/3',0,1),('1/3',1,2)],checks=[dict(kind='transformed_probability',lower=0,upper='3/2',answer='5/6')])])]
